{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Possessor of heaven and earth.</b> One should not understand קונה as 'purchasing from a seller,' but rather the meaning of קונה is 'acquiring,' in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory in many places: קנה חברו — that is, he acquired for himself; like the expression המגביה מציאה לחברו קנה חברו — the intent there is not that he purchased from another:" "<b>And know</b> that everything existent is subject to acquisition, but the 'nothingness' (אין) is not subject to acquisition. And since He, blessed be He, brought the world forth from nothingness into existence, Scripture employs the language of acquisition: קונה שמים וארץ. And if so, the explanation of the word קונה is that He assigned them to Israel:" "<b>But</b> in Bereshit Rabbah it appears that the Sages understood the word קונה according to its plain sense — that He purchased from another, and that other is the first matter (חומר) from which He created everything. And that is what they expounded: ״קונה שמים וארץ״ — from whom did He purchase? As in this saying: פלניא עינוהי יאי שערוהי לא יאי — 'a certain person's eyes are beautiful, his hair is not beautiful?' — in astonishment. And the explanation of this is that from the fact that he praised the eyes, you should not infer from this that his hair is not beautiful, for he is entirely beautiful; rather, the recounting of praise came only with regard to the matter he was speaking of. And likewise in the creation of the world, when it says קונה — that He purchased from the matter, you should not infer from this that that matter was pre-existent with Him from the beginning, for even the matter He created anew, and He renewed it from nothingness:" "<b>And he blessed him,</b> and said: ברוך אברם וגו׳ וברוך אל עליון. Malki-Tzedek placed the blessing of Avraham before the blessing of El Elyon, and sinned in this, for he should have placed the blessing of HaShem first, in the manner we find with David, peace be upon him (1 Samuel 25:32-33): ברוך ה׳ אלהי ישראל אשר שלחך היום הזה לקראתי וברוך טעמך וברוכה את וגו׳. And his punishment was that the priesthood was taken from him and given to Avraham. That is what David said in the psalm (Psalms 110:1): נאם ה׳ לאדני שב לימיני — which is founded upon Avraham, whom David called 'my lord' (אדני) because he was his ancestor. He said: נשבע ה׳ ולא ינחם אתה כהן לעולם על דברתי מלכי צדק — that is, on account of that utterance which was unfitting, in which he placed the blessing of HaShem, blessed be He, second. And the fact that Yehudah sentenced Tamar to burning, she being the daughter of Shem — he did not know that the priesthood had been taken from him:" "<b>And according to</b> the opinion of the Midrash, קונה שמים וארץ refers back to Avraham, for thus they said in the Midrash Mishlei (Proverbs 17:1) on the verse טוב פת חרבה ושלוה בה: Avraham merited acquiring heaven and earth, as it is said: ברוך אברם לאל עליון קונה שמים וארץ — for his sons merited to inherit the Torah, which was given from the heavens, and earth, for his sons merited to be fruitful and multiply like the dust of the earth:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {2 "The Aramaic anecdote (פלניא עינוהי יאי שערוהי לא יאי) is rendered hyper-literally; the parenthetical gloss preserves the rhetorical force of the remonstrative question as understood from context.", 3 "Rabbeinu Bahya writes הש\"י (השם יתברך), rendered as 'HaShem, blessed be He,' matching his reverent usage; the divine name abbreviations ה' and אדני in the Psalm quotations are preserved in Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:36.628827387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}